{
  "email": "hannah.lee@brightline.example",
  "reason": "KB-008 quick check: see whether similar messages were caught"
}

{
  "summary": "Reported: Hannah Lee (Payroll Specialist, Finance, user_id 12) got an email from someone calling themselves 'IT Support' asking her to reply with her password to \"finish setting up\" her account. She says she did NOT reply. She did not mention clicking a link or opening an attachment. What I checked: account is active, 0 failed logins, not blocked. must_change_password=true (password last set 2026-10-11), so this looks like a newly set up account, which fits the pretext. Her spam quarantine is empty, so the message got past filtering and is sitting in her inbox. Still needed: security review of the sender address, headers, and subject (I don't have these yet; the user has been asked to leave the email in her mailbox). Block the sender tenant-wide if it's malicious, and check whether other users got it, especially in Finance/Payroll. If it came from an internal account, treat that account as possibly compromised. Also confirm with the user that she didn't click any links.",
  "reason": "KB-008: all reported suspicious emails get escalated so a person can review the sender and block it"
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Hannah, thanks for checking with us first, and well done for not replying. That email is not from us. Brightline IT will never ask for your password by email or in any other way. Please don't reply to it, click any links in it, or forward it to colleagues. Just leave it in your mailbox for now so our security team can look at it. I've passed it to them. They'll check the sender, block it if needed, and may contact you for details. If you did click a link or type your password anywhere because of this email, please tell us straight away so we can secure your account.",
  "internal_note": "KB-008 suspicious email report. User says she did not reply and didn't mention clicking anything. Account is active, 0 failed logins. must_change_password=true, so this is likely a new account, which fits the 'finish setting up your account' story. Quarantine is empty, so the message reached her inbox. Escalated to Tier 2/security (escalation 1) to review the sender and headers, block it tenant-wide if malicious, and check whether others got it, especially in Payroll/Finance. We don't have the sender address or subject yet. The user was asked to keep the email in her mailbox.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
